From: abdulaziz <alanziabdulaziz4@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 06:30:45 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] tests: roll back only onto planes the last ubatch wrote
 in test-recurrent-state-rollback

Two steps rolled back across earlier micro-batches, which the memory never
snapshots (see llama_memory_seq_rm): the partial-flags checkpoint step replayed
three positions one token at a time and then rolled the same three back -- on
a6536c3 that passed only because plane 3, untouched by the T = 1 replays, still
held the prompt ubatch's state for the identical tokens; since 317e4d0 it is
refused (rs_n_valid == 0) and the test exited with 'partial rollback failed'.
The multi-seq sub-test rolled back exactly the 3 tokens of a 3-token ubatch and
read plane 3 of the 16-token prefill ubatch (the state after position 12, not
15) on every revision since the upstream merge.

Partial step: extend both contexts with the same n_rollback + 1 tokens in ONE
ubatch and roll back n_rollback, the largest rollback that ubatch supports;
replay positions ext_pos + 1 .. ext_pos + 3 (still crossing DSV4's ratio-4
boundary). The 'full' replay logits are kept for the dirty-context check.

Multi-seq: ctx_roll decodes [0, 15) then [15, 19) as one 4-token ubatch and
rolls back 3 (plane 3 = state after 15); ctx_ref is restored from each seq's
checkpoint at that point, which resolves the pending plane into a plain state,
so both contexts replay from bit-identical bytes and the 1e-7 tolerance stays
valid by construction. A forward-only reference cannot be compared bitwise: an
honest rollback target sits inside a multi-token ubatch of ctx_roll and the CPU
matmul kernels depend on the ubatch size (sgemm rejects small n).

Not run here (model binaries are off limits in this session); established by
construction from the kernel slot mapping and the memory's plane indexing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
